description:
The keras package as a number of helper functions for doing data cleaning for neural networks, including pad_sequences, and texts_to_sequences. These functions seems to pass the R data to python which adjusts the data, then returns it to R. Unfortunately, these functions take a very long time to run. Long enough that I haven't been able to use them for more than very small problems. This is doubly unfortunate because it seems like you could write pure R functions that can quickly do the task. For example, consider pad_sequences
```r
pad_sequences_fast <- function(x, maxlen, padding="pre", truncating ="pre", value=0){
  x %>%
    map(function(x){
      if(length(x) > maxlen){
        if(truncating == "pre"){
          x[(length(x)-(maxlen)+1):length(x)]
        } else if(truncating == "post") {
          x[1:maxlen]
        } else {
          stop("Invalid value for 'truncating'")
        }
      } else {
        if(padding == "pre"){
          c(rep(value, maxlen-length(x)),x)
        } else if(padding == "post"){
          c(x,rep(value, maxlen-length(x)))
        } else {
          stop("Invalid value for 'padding'")
        }
      }
    }) %>%
    do.call(c,.) %>%
    unlist() %>%
    matrix(ncol=maxlen,byrow=TRUE)
}

maxlen <- 10
num_samples <- 15000
sample_data <- purrr::map(1:num_samples,~as.list(rep(1,sample(1:maxlen,1))))

system.time({
  keras::pad_sequences(sample_data,maxlen)
})
system.time({
  pad_sequences_fast(sample_data,maxlen)
})
```

The results are really different!
```
> system.time({
+   keras::pad_sequences(sample_data,maxlen)
+ })
   user  system elapsed 
  11.36    0.03   11.51 
> system.time({
+   pad_sequences_fast(sample_data,maxlen)
+ })
   user  system elapsed 
   0.06    0.00    0.06 
```

It seems like if I was able to make pure R helper functions without too much of as hassle, it might be worth having pure R versions in the library itself.

Currently, `keras_model_custom()` doesn't seem to fully support the [model subclassing API](https://www.tensorflow.org/guide/keras#model_subclassing), in the sense that it only accommodates a forward pass function. Therefore, in order to implement something like https://colab.research.google.com/github/tensorflow/docs/blob/master/site/en/r2/tutorials/generative/cvae.ipynb#scrollTo=VGLbvBEmjK0a, we'd have to define multiple top level functions each with a call to `keras_mode_custom()` (e.g. https://blogs.rstudio.com/tensorflow/posts/2019-01-24-vq-vae/).

Could we consider exposing an API that would allow the user to encapsulate methods in the model object? E.g. something like
```
# signature of the new function (or we could use keras_model_custom and try to maintain compatibility)
keras_model_bespoke <- function(..., init = NULL, name = NULL) {...}

model <- keras_model_bespoke(
  init = function(self) {
    self$dense1 <- layer_dense(units = 32, activation = "relu")
    self$dense2 <- layer_dense(units = 10, activation = "softmax")
  },
  call = function(inputs, training = FALSE) {
    x <- self$dense1(inputs)
    self$dense2(x)
  },
  some_other_fun = ...
)
```
`keras_model_bespoke()` would return a model object, and defining a constructor would be similar to the current `keras_model_custom()` API, e.g.
```
custom_model_constructor <- function(...) {
  # ...
  keras_model_bespoke(...)
}
model <- custom_model_constructor()
```

When using custom models that have certain layers (e.g., dropout, batchnorm), we often need to be able to pass a ```training_phase``` argument to the respective layers.
For example:

```
model <-
  function(name = NULL) {
    keras_model_custom(name = name, function(self) {
      self$fc1 <- layer_dense(units = 7 * 7 * 64, use_bias = FALSE)
      self$batchnorm1 <- layer_batch_normalization()
      self$relu1 <- layer_activation_relu()
      
      function(inputs,
               mask = NULL,
               training = TRUE) {
        self$fc1(inputs) %>%
          self$batchnorm1(training = training) %>%
          self$relu1() 
      }
    })
  }
```
As far as I see this is not possible with the current implementation (if I'm not getting it wrong :-))

Found this one while testing `cloudml`, I can take a look:

